00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:27.460
This is Dactylochlamys pisciformis, and it
is one of the rarest organisms we have ever found.

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:32.560
It’s only been reported four times since
it was first discovered in 1901.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:40.399
And here is what we know about it: Dactylochlamys
pisciformis is a ciliate with a single contractile

00:00:40.399 --> 00:00:44.120
vacuole, long cilia, and rigid tentacle-like
spikes.

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:49.539
That’s not a whole lot of information, and
some of that you could probably have guessed

00:00:49.539 --> 00:00:51.629
just by watching it work.

00:00:51.629 --> 00:00:56.829
And that’s kind of a dilemma for us because
we really want to show you a lot more of the

00:00:56.829 --> 00:01:02.489
Dactylochlamys pisciformis, but it’s also
important to us to be able to say something

00:01:02.489 --> 00:01:06.810
about it, to fit it into a story about the
microcosmos.

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:11.140
But science doesn’t care about the story
you want to tell.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:13.759
If the organism is rare, it’s rare.

00:01:13.759 --> 00:01:18.810
There’s probably not a lot that we know
about it, and there’s not a lot we can do about that.

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:25.630
But the microcosmos is full of mysteries like
this one, with red herrings and uncanny resemblances

00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:27.689
that create even more confusion.

00:01:27.689 --> 00:01:32.450
And when you’ve spent as much time in this
world as James, our master of microscopes

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:38.979
has, the information you gather offers clues...but
you may still have to wait to close the case.

00:01:38.979 --> 00:01:49.729
So today, we’re going to talk about a few
of those unsolved mysteries.

00:01:49.729 --> 00:01:52.000
Like this ciliate spinning around.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:58.020
It looks kind of like a transparent gourd
that got dip-dyed in a light yellow paint.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:03.970
James found it in one of his marine tanks
that he stocks with samples gathered from the beach.

00:02:03.970 --> 00:02:07.580
And when he saw it, he couldn’t figure out
what the heck it was.

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:10.510
So he went to his mentor, and she didn’t
know either.

00:02:10.510 --> 00:02:16.480
But you don’t get to be a protistologist
by giving up easily, so they looked everywhere

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:20.340
to figure out the identity of their mysterious
ciliate.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:21.870
One place to start?

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:23.480
The mouth.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:27.460
Ciliate mouths can tell you a lot about what
group they belong to.

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:31.580
But it’s hard to make out the shape of the
mouth when you’ve got an organism spinning

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:38.040
around, so James slowed it down by supporting
the weight of the coverslip with petroleum

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:46.099
jelly and then pressing down very, very slowly,
very, very carefully until the organism was

00:02:46.099 --> 00:02:48.620
safely immobilized.

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:53.990
And from there, James and his mentor were
able to document the shape of the cell’s

00:02:53.990 --> 00:02:59.320
mouth and nucleus, along with a lot of other
tiny details that helped them finally identify

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:02.610
the ciliate as Paraspathidium.

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:05.159
So great, mystery solved, right?

00:03:05.159 --> 00:03:07.280
Yeah, of course not.

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:14.030
There are, as far as we know, two species
of Paraspathidium: Paraspathidium fuscum was

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:20.670
first discovered by the noted German microbiologist
Alfred Kahl in 1928, and the second species

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:28.480
was discovered in 2009 by scientists in northern
China, who named it Paraspathidium apofuscum2.

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:33.480
So we have two possibilities, and between
them, the features of the Paraspathidium apofuscum

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:38.430
seem to be the better match for our mystery
Paraspathidium.

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:43.410
And if that’s the case, that means we have
found a Parasapthidium apofuscum.

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:48.340
And that would make this the first time the
species has ever been seen in Europe!

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:51.630
Except…the label doesn’t seem to fit fully.

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:56.860
There’s a region on some ciliates called
the “dorsal brush,” and while we don’t

00:03:56.860 --> 00:04:00.849
know the function of that structure, we know
that it’s diverse enough to distinguish

00:04:00.849 --> 00:04:02.459
between different species.

00:04:02.459 --> 00:04:08.709
And our species’ dorsal brush is way shorter
than the other Paraspathidium species that

00:04:08.709 --> 00:04:10.430
have been described.

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:16.920
So that means that we have a third option
here: this could be a whole new species.

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:22.520
Now, we don’t know for certain, but hopefully
one day we will know if we here at Journey

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:28.160
to the Microcosmos have expanded the Paraspathidium
family just a little bit.

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:34.539
Now for our next mystery: this is a frontonia,
and from a distance, there’s probably not

00:04:34.539 --> 00:04:36.830
anything particularly notable about it.

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:41.220
It’s swimming and hitting little bits of
debris, which is pretty much the lifestyle

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:43.510
of a lot of microbes.

00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:48.729
But when you increase the magnification, you
can see that many of its vacuoles are packed

00:04:48.729 --> 00:04:49.820
with tiny rods.

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:54.320
If you’ve been with us for a while, that
maybe looks a little bit familiar…maybe

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:59.560
a bit like the paramecium we’ve found that
were infected with a bacteria called holospora.

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:03.790
At first glance, this resemblance isn’t
too surprising.

00:05:03.790 --> 00:05:08.520
Frontonia and paramecia are both ciliates,
and we found this infected Frontonia in the

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:12.960
same pond where we’ve found our holospora-infected
Paramecium.

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:19.140
But this is the first ever record of a holospora-like
infection in Frontonia.

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:24.680
And unlike paramecium, where the holospora
infect the nuclei, these bacteria are targeting

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:29.470
the vacuoles all over the frontonia’s cytoplasm.

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:35.060
Some of the frontonia were so damaged by the
infection that it was hard to even recognize the organism.

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:38.509
So what is infecting this frontonia?

00:05:38.509 --> 00:05:43.759
Well, we know it’s a bacteria, and that
bacteria is definitely holospora-like.

00:05:43.759 --> 00:05:49.230
But outside of that resemblance, we do not
know.

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:58.280
And now for our final mystery, let’s circle
on back to the Dactylochlamys pisciformis.

00:05:58.280 --> 00:06:03.569
Like we said at the beginning of the episode,
this organism is super rare, so finding it

00:06:03.569 --> 00:06:09.980
was an amazing opportunity to be able to study
it more and see what else we can learn about

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:14.270
its behavior and its relationships to other
ciliates.

00:06:14.270 --> 00:06:22.190
So James took very good care of it and got
over 20 hours of footage over the course of a week.

00:06:22.190 --> 00:06:28.170
He observed that it seemed to be almost motionless
in the dark, but once he turned on the light,

00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:32.100
the Dactylochlamys pisciformis would start
swimming around.

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:37.710
And it also seems to use its cilia like little
whiskers to sense the environment around it.

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:44.960
But weirdly, in all that time, James never
saw it eat.

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:52.370
And then one day, he came back to check on
his slide, and the Dactylochlamys was no longer there.

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:59.979
It just disappeared, taking all its secrets
and opportunities with it and leaving only

00:06:59.979 --> 00:07:04.530
the footage James took for us to remember
it by.

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:08.410
We have no idea where it went or what happened
to it.

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:14.069
We hope there’s a good story there, but
this time, we’re not the ones who get to tell it.

00:07:14.069 --> 00:07:19.509
Reality doesn’t care about our videos, and
the amount of stuff that we don’t know as

00:07:19.509 --> 00:07:24.600
individuals and as a species dwarfs what we
do know.

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:31.180
That is a frustration, but also, it is a remarkable
opportunity.

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:42.500
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

